a run aborted by a non-StandardError
  drains the nesting stack either way
  an exception that indicates a fault in the action (SystemStackError, ScriptError, …)
    settles into an exception-outcome result that .call returns rather than raising
    still raises through call!, as the original object
    reports it once, so a runaway recursion is no longer invisible to error tracking
    fires on_error alongside on_exception (the documented superset), and neither on_failure nor the success path
    records an honest exception outcome rather than leaving the run reading as a success
    logs the completion line at the exception outcome, not as a success
    covers the whole ScriptError family, not just SystemStackError
    settles the same way when raised from a before hook
    settles the same way when raised from an around hook
